SnapRAID RAID engine comparison notes
=====================================


Scope
-----

These notes compare the SnapRAID RAID engine with other RAID and erasure-coding
implementations that are useful optimization references. The goal is not to
rank projects by a single benchmark. The goal is to understand which design
choices follow from each workload and which techniques can or cannot be
transferred to SnapRAID.

The most important rule is the same one used in NOTES: optimize the real
SnapRAID workload, not only isolated inner loops. A technique that reduces
instruction count can still be a loss if it adds another pass over a 256 KiB
block, increases cache footprint, or changes the lifetime of parity data in an
unfavorable way.

Three different kinds of advantage are distinguished throughout this document:

* Algorithmic advantage: the coding or recovery algorithm fundamentally
  requires less work.

* Implementation advantage: the same mathematical work is scheduled,
  vectorized, or dispatched more efficiently.

* Workload-specific advantage: a design is faster because its assumptions fit
  one application, but the same design may be unsuitable for another
  application.

The third category is especially important. Linux RAID6, OpenZFS RAIDZ, ISA-L,
klauspost/reedsolomon, Jerasure/GF-Complete, and CM256/gf256 do not all
optimize the same problem.


