Compared implementations
------------------------

SnapRAID: A storage-oriented RAID engine specialized for large fixed-size
          blocks and a small parity count. The current implementation supports
          one to six parity levels and is aggressively specialized by parity
          count and CPU backend.

* Typical data/parity block size is 256 KiB.

* Generation tries to read a source region once, advance all required parity
  outputs together, keep accumulators in SIMD registers, and store final parity
  once.

* P is XOR. Q uses a mode-specific structured recurrence. The normal RAID mode
  uses powers of generator 2 over polynomial 0x11d. The AES mode uses the G23
  sequence over polynomial 0x11b, with multiply-by-2 transitions and fixed
  multiply-by-3 transitions between its five cosets. Higher parity rows use
  the corresponding extended Cauchy construction.

* Non-GFNI AES generation exploits this structure with separate x2-only and
  G23 kernels. Small arrays that do not reach an x3 boundary use the simple
  x2 path directly instead of carrying G23 control flow through the hot loop.

* Generation has specialized GEN1..GEN6 kernels. Recovery has dedicated REC1,
  REC2, and RECX paths plus important fast cases such as rec1of1.

* Current optimized backends include x86 SSSE3, AVX2, AVX512BW and GFNI, plus
  AArch64 and AArch32 NEON.

* Generation uses non-temporal parity stores where appropriate because parity
  normally enters a queue before the disk writer consumes it.

Linux RAID6: A kernel RAID6 P+Q engine. It is much less general than a
             Reed-Solomon library and therefore can specialize very
             aggressively for exactly two parity equations.

* P is XOR and Q is the classic RAID6 generator-power syndrome over GF(2^8),
  implemented naturally with a Horner multiply-by-2 recurrence.

* Generation keeps P and Q in registers while walking all data disks for the
  current vector position, then writes both parity vectors once.

* x86 generation contains spatial x1/x2/x4 variants, explicit prefetch, and
  non-temporal final stores in optimized paths.

* Recovery is specifically designed for one or two failures. The public RAID6
  recovery interface operates on at most PAGE_SIZE at a time.

* Architecture coverage is broad: x86, ARM NEON, PowerPC, s390, RISC-V vector,
  LoongArch SIMD, and generic integer implementations.

* Generation can be benchmarked at boot and the fastest registered
  implementation selected dynamically.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: A filesystem-integrated RAID engine supporting RAIDZ1, RAIDZ2
               and RAIDZ3. It must handle ZFS RAIDZ geometry, ABD buffers, and
               rows whose columns may have different lengths.

* P, Q and R use coefficients based on 1, 2 and 4 respectively over the
  standard RAID GF(2^8) polynomial.

* Q and R generation use fixed multiply-by-2 and multiply-by-4 Horner
  recurrences.

* Parity generation is blocked in 64 KiB regions for L2 locality.

* For each data column, the parity region is loaded, updated and stored. Cached
  stores are therefore a natural consequence of parity reuse.

* Recovery has explicit rec_p, rec_q, rec_r, rec_pq, rec_pr, rec_qr and rec_pqr
  operations with coefficients prepared ahead of the hot loops.

* The implementation can benchmark each generation and recovery operation
  independently and assemble a per-operation fastest backend.

Intel ISA-L: A high-throughput general erasure-coding library. The fundamental
             operation is a GF(2^8) vector dot product with arbitrary
             coefficients, not a fixed RAID P/Q/R layout.

* The API supports systematic Reed-Solomon matrices, Cauchy matrices, generic
  matrix inversion, and user-provided coefficient sets.

* Fast encode/decode kernels calculate one to six destination vectors in one
  pass. More outputs are handled in groups.

* For a source vector, SIMD kernels can split the source nibbles once and reuse
  them while updating several output accumulators.

* PSHUFB-style paths use 32 bytes of expanded low/high nibble tables per
  coefficient.

* x86 backends include SSE/AVX/AVX2/AVX512 and GFNI variants. Current non-x86
  support includes AArch64 NEON and SVE/SVE2, PPC64LE VSX, and RISC-V RVV.

* A separate update API supports adding the contribution of one source vector
  to one or more existing parity vectors.

klauspost/reedsolomon: A modern general-purpose Reed-Solomon implementation in
                       Go with architecture-specific generated kernels,
                       cache-aware work partitioning, parallel execution, and a
                       separate Leopard mode for very high shard counts.

* The normal Reed-Solomon path uses a systematic matrix, with Vandermonde as
  the default and optional Cauchy, Jerasure-compatible, PAR1 and custom
  matrices.

* Generated SIMD microkernels cover combinations of multiple inputs and
  multiple outputs, up to 10 by 10 on the main generated paths.

* The implementation chooses a cache-sized amount of data per round instead of
  blindly processing an arbitrarily large shard in one unit.

* x86 paths include AVX2, AVX512 and GFNI. AArch64 includes NEON and SVE; there
  is also architecture-specific PPC64LE support.

* Normal reconstruction builds a decoding matrix from surviving shards, inverts
  it, and reuses the same multi-output coding machinery for missing shards.

* An inversion-tree cache can reuse decoding information for repeated erasure
  patterns.

* For shard counts beyond the normal GF(2^8) matrix regime, Leopard provides a
  different O(n log n) coding strategy.

Jerasure/GF-Complete: A general erasure-coding framework paired with a generic
                      Galois-field library. Jerasure provides coding matrices,
                      bitmatrices, schedules and decode orchestration;
                      GF-Complete provides field arithmetic and region
                      operations.

* Jerasure supports Vandermonde and Cauchy coding constructions and can convert
  matrices into XOR-oriented bitmatrices.

* Cauchy good-matrix and smart-schedule techniques primarily reduce XOR work in
  bitmatrix/packetized coding, not direct SIMD byte-wise GF multiplication.

* For w=8, GF-Complete can use low/high nibble split tables with SSSE3 or NEON,
  closely resembling the PSHUFB/TBL multiplication technique used by other
  libraries.

* A GF-Complete region multiply keeps the coefficient tables resident while
  processing the whole region, but Jerasure matrix encoding naturally invokes
  such region work separately for matrix rows/sources.

* The Ceph mirror snapshots used here are legacy code and should be read as
  algorithm/implementation references rather than current optimization targets.

CM256/gf256: A compact Cauchy MDS codec paired with a GF(2^8) region engine. It
             targets generic original/recovery blocks and can support many more
             recovery blocks than SnapRAID.

* CM256 normalizes its Cauchy construction so that the first recovery row is
  all ones, giving a simple XOR parity row.

* Encoding processes each recovery block as a whole-region multiply plus
  multiply-add sequence.

* gf256 uses low/high nibble shuffle multiplication on SSSE3/AVX2 and NEON
  paths.

* The standalone AVX2 gf256 tables are pre-expanded to vector width, trading a
  larger table footprint for normal vector loads instead of 128-bit
  load-and-broadcast operations.

* CM256 decoding exploits Cauchy structure directly through a Schur-type LDU
  solve rather than treating every decode as an arbitrary matrix inversion.


Primary target and workload
---------------------------

The primary target is the most important comparison category. It explains many
apparent contradictions between otherwise good implementations.

SnapRAID: Large storage blocks, normally 256 KiB, and one to six parity levels.
          The normal generation workload streams many MiB of source data per
          stripe and queues parity before disk output. Full extra passes over
          source or parity buffers are expensive enough to influence kernel
          design.

Linux RAID6: Kernel RAID6 with exactly P+Q. Generation is a streaming storage
             operation, but the recovery interface is page-sized. The tiny
             fixed parity count permits formulas and kernels that would not
             generalize to many parity outputs.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Filesystem RAID with one to three parity columns, dynamic row
               geometry and ABD buffers. Cache-blocked processing and repeated
               parity updates are reasonable because the parity working region
               is deliberately kept hot.

ISA-L: General high-throughput erasure coding for storage, networking and other
       buffer-oriented applications. It must handle arbitrary coefficient
       matrices and parity counts without assuming RAID-specific P/Q semantics.

klauspost: General Reed-Solomon shards over a wide range of block sizes and
           data/parity counts. It combines generic matrix semantics with
           cache-aware tiling, parallelism and generated SIMD microkernels.

Jerasure/GF: General erasure-coding construction and field operations. The
             framework also supports packetized bitmatrix/schedule approaches
             whose cost model is different from direct byte-wise SIMD GF
             multiplication.

CM256/gf256: Generic Cauchy MDS blocks, including use cases with many recovery
             blocks. Whole-region GF operations make coefficient residency
             easy, but can require source/output regions to be traversed more
             than once.

The direct consequence for SnapRAID is:

    Saving arithmetic is not enough.

    An optimization should preferably preserve:

        one traversal of each large source region
        simultaneous progress of required parity outputs
        accumulator residency in SIMD registers
        one final parity store


Coding scheme and parity organization
-------------------------------------

SnapRAID: Systematic storage layout with P as XOR and higher parity rows from
          an extended Cauchy construction. Q has a structured recurrence whose
          exact form depends on the selected field.

          In the RAID mode, Q uses powers of generator 2 and therefore has the
          classic fixed multiply-by-2 Horner recurrence.

          In the AES mode, Q uses the G23 sequence. Its Horner recurrence is
          still highly structured: multiplication is by 2 almost everywhere,
          with multiplication by 3 only at four fixed coset boundaries.

          These coefficient properties and the small maximum parity count are
          known to the implementation and can be exploited directly.

Linux RAID6: Exactly P+Q. P is XOR and Q uses powers of generator 2 over the
             standard RAID field. Q therefore has a natural Horner recurrence
             with a fixed multiply-by-2 operation.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: RAIDZ1/2/3 use P, Q and R. P uses coefficient 1, Q uses powers
               of 2, and R uses powers of 4. The fixed 1/2/4 choice supports
               simple XOR, MUL2 and MUL4 operations in the generation kernels.

ISA-L: Generic systematic Reed-Solomon or Cauchy matrices, plus arbitrary
       caller-provided coefficients. The vector kernels do not depend on a
       particular RAID matrix.

klauspost: Systematic matrix coding with Vandermonde by default and multiple
           matrix options. Generated kernels treat the matrix coefficients
           generically while recognizing selected useful matrix properties in
           some paths.

Jerasure/GF: Generic Vandermonde, Cauchy and bitmatrix constructions. Cauchy
             matrix improvement can minimize bitmatrix one-count, which matters
             for XOR schedules but not directly for PSHUFB/GFNI byte
             multiplication cost.

CM256/gf256: Normalized Cauchy coding. The normalization deliberately creates
             an all-one first recovery row. Remaining rows are generic Cauchy
             coefficients rather than a RAID-specific Q power sequence.

The matrix choice therefore affects more than recoverability. SnapRAID, Linux
RAID6 and OpenZFS can exploit structured generator recurrences in generation.

Linux RAID6 and OpenZFS use simple fixed multipliers throughout their Horner
recurrences. SnapRAID RAID does the same for Q with multiply-by-2, while
SnapRAID AES uses a piecewise-fixed G23 recurrence with only four known
multiply-by-3 boundaries.

This remains fundamentally different from generic matrix libraries, which
spend more effort making arbitrary coefficient multiplication fast.


GF(2^8) arithmetic
------------------

Most implementations compared here use byte-oriented GF(2^8), but they do not
all use the same coding matrix or even the same primitive polynomial. Similar
SIMD instructions do not imply compatible parity.

SnapRAID: Two GF(2^8) fields are supported by the Cauchy modes.

          The normal RAID field uses polynomial 0x11d. Non-GFNI SIMD
          multiplication uses compact low/high nibble shuffle tables. RAID
          GFNI represents multiplication by each coefficient as an 8-byte
          affine matrix because the native GFNI multiply instruction is defined
          for the AES field.

          The AES field uses polynomial 0x11b, matching the field implemented
          natively by VGF2P8MULB. AES GFNI can therefore multiply directly by
          coefficient bytes without the affine conversion required by the RAID
          field.

          Non-GFNI AES generation uses the same SIMD field primitives as the
          rest of the implementation, while the structured G23 Q row is
          evaluated with multiply-by-2 Horner transitions and multiply-by-3
          only at the fixed coset boundaries.

Linux RAID6: Uses polynomial 0x11d. Generation mostly needs multiplication by
             the fixed generator 2, implemented by byte shift plus conditional
             XOR with 0x1d. Recovery also uses precomputed GF tables and SIMD
             shuffle tables.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Uses polynomial 0x11d. Generation emphasizes fixed MUL2 and
               MUL4. General recovery coefficient multiplication uses
               table/shuffle machinery in optimized SIMD backends.

ISA-L: Uses the standard 0x11d field for its common GF(2^8) erasure-code
       functions. Classic SIMD paths expand each coefficient into 16-byte low
       and 16-byte high nibble tables. GFNI paths use a GFNI-ready expanded
       representation.

klauspost: The normal Reed-Solomon path uses the same common 0x11d field.
           Generated backends include shuffle-based and GFNI multiplication,
           allowing a particularly direct microarchitectural comparison with
           SnapRAID.

Jerasure/GF: GF-Complete is intentionally more general and supports multiple
             field techniques. Its w=8 split-table SIMD path uses the same
             basic low/high nibble decomposition as PSHUFB-based SnapRAID and
             ISA-L kernels.

CM256/gf256: Uses a different GF(2^8) polynomial, 0x14d, in its normal
             configuration. The SIMD multiplication technique is still
             comparable even though the actual coding coefficients and parity
             bytes are not compatible with SnapRAID.


Supported architectures
-----------------------

Architecture lists below describe the RAID/erasure-code engines studied, not
every instruction set used elsewhere in each project.

SnapRAID: Portable scalar C plus x86 SSSE3, AVX2, AVX512BW and GFNI, and NEON
          on both AArch64 and AArch32.

Linux RAID6: Generic integer plus x86 MMX/SSE/SSE2/AVX2/AVX512 generation and
             SSSE3/AVX2/AVX512 recovery. It also has ARM NEON, PowerPC
             AltiVec/vpermxor, s390 vector, RISC-V RVV and LoongArch SIMD
             support.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Scalar, x86-64 SSE2, SSSE3, AVX2, AVX512F and AVX512BW, AArch64
               NEON and NEONx2, and PowerPC AltiVec in the current RAIDZ math
               dispatcher.

ISA-L: x86-64 SSE/AVX/AVX2/AVX512 including GFNI, AArch64 NEON and SVE/SVE2,
       PPC64LE VSX, RISC-V64 RVV, and portable baseline C.

klauspost: Portable Go plus generated x86 SIMD including AVX2/AVX512/GFNI,
           AArch64 NEON/SVE, and architecture-specific PPC64LE support.

Jerasure/GF: Portable C with GF-Complete SIMD support including SSSE3 and NEON,
             plus other generic field techniques. It predates the AVX512/GFNI
             generation of libraries.

CM256/gf256: Portable scalar code plus gf256 SIMD implementations centered on
             SSSE3, AVX2 and NEON.

For architecture breadth, Linux RAID6 is the strongest reference. For modern
generic GF kernels, ISA-L and klauspost are more directly comparable with
current SnapRAID x86 and ARM work.


Kernel and code specialization strategy
---------------------------------------

SnapRAID: Hand-specializes around the small fixed parity domain. GEN1 through
          GEN6 are different kernels, and recovery distinguishes REC1, REC2,
          RECX and specific fast paths. This allows register allocation and
          algebra to change with parity count.

          Non-GFNI AES generation is additionally specialized into separate
          x2-only and G23 kernels. This is deliberate code duplication:
          keeping G23 boundary control flow out of the common x2-only path was
          measured to improve generated code and throughput even when no x3
          transition would actually execute.

Linux RAID6: Specializes a single mathematical problem, P+Q, but supplies
             several spatial unroll factors and architecture backends. The
             x1/x2/x4 variants are alternative implementations of the same
             fixed syndrome operation.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Specializes by P, PQ and PQR generation and by each supported
               recovery combination. Backend macro layers provide the same
               algorithm with different SIMD widths and field primitives.

ISA-L: Provides families of assembly kernels for one through six simultaneous
       destinations. The high-level function partitions a larger destination
       count into groups supported by those kernels.

klauspost: Uses extensive code generation for many N-input by M-output
           combinations. This moves specialization from RAID semantics to
           generic matrix microkernels and creates a large generated assembly
           body.

Jerasure/GF: Keeps coding construction and GF region primitives relatively
             separate. The framework is general, while GF-Complete specializes
             the field operation selected for a region.

CM256/gf256: Uses a compact generic Cauchy codec and optimized region
             primitives rather than a large family of parity-count-specific
             encode kernels.

SnapRAID and klauspost therefore demonstrate two different kinds of
specialization:

    SnapRAID:
        specialize the RAID algorithm

    klauspost/reedsolomon:
        specialize generic matrix multiplication microkernels


Memory traversal
----------------

Memory traversal is the category where workload differences matter most for
SnapRAID. For large blocks, the number of full source and destination passes
can dominate small instruction-count savings.

SnapRAID: The preferred organization is vector-offset outer, source-disk inner,
          with all required parity accumulators live. A source chunk is loaded
          once and contributes to all parity outputs before the next source is
          loaded.

    for each SIMD chunk:
        initialize P/Q/R/... accumulators
        for each source disk:
            load source chunk once
            update all parity accumulators
        store each final parity chunk once

Linux RAID6: Very similar for P+Q generation. P and Q stay live while the
             kernel walks all data disks for the current SIMD chunk, followed
             by one final store of each parity output.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Processes 64 KiB cache-blocked regions. Within a region, each
               data column updates parity buffers that are loaded and stored
               repeatedly. This deliberately trades more parity memory
               operations for L2 locality and compatibility with RAIDZ/ABD
               geometry.

    for each 64 KiB region:
        initialize parity region
        for each data column:
            load data
            load/update/store P
            load/update/store Q/R as required

ISA-L: Multi-output dot-product kernels load a source vector chunk, split it
       once, and apply several coefficient tables to several live destination
       accumulators. This preserves much of the same source-reuse benefit as
       SnapRAID without assuming RAID-specific coefficients.

klauspost: Uses cache-aware rounds and multi-input/multi-output microkernels.
           It does not rely on one global whole-shard traversal policy; it
           chooses working-set sizes intended to keep the active inputs and
           outputs useful in cache.

Jerasure/GF: A GF-Complete multiply_region keeps one coefficient table resident
             over a complete region. Jerasure matrix encoding naturally invokes
             region operations for matrix rows and source contributions, so a
             source region can be revisited for different outputs.

CM256/gf256: Encoding one recovery block at a time also gives excellent
             coefficient residency, but producing several recovery blocks
             requires separate whole-region work for those outputs.

For SnapRAID, copying the Jerasure/CM256 whole-region orientation would
normally be the wrong trade. It could improve coefficient residency while
increasing 256 KiB source rereads. Current GEN2/GEN3 table residency shows that
SnapRAID can obtain part of the coefficient-residency benefit without changing
the single-pass multi-output traversal.


Generation strategy
-------------------

SnapRAID P: Pure XOR and a dedicated GEN1 path.

SnapRAID Q: Generator-power structure. GFNI RAID paths use Horner with a fixed
            multiply-by-2 affine matrix where register pressure permits it.
            Other backends use the strategy that benchmarks best for that
            parity count.

SnapRAID 3..6: Higher extended-Cauchy rows use direct coefficient
               multiplication. Current GEN2/GEN3 optimizations preload
               coefficient tables or matrices into vector registers when the
               register file permits it.

Linux RAID6: P and Q are fused. Q is always the fixed Horner recurrence. The
             result is exceptionally compact because there is no requirement to
             support arbitrary third or later parity rows.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: P, PQ and PQR have separate operations. Q and R exploit fixed
               MUL2 and MUL4 recurrences, but parity buffers are updated in
               cache as each data column is processed.

ISA-L: Treats generation as generic matrix dot products. Up to six output rows
       are fused, so one source load and one nibble split can feed several
       parity accumulators.

klauspost: Treats generation as generic matrix coding but uses generated NxM
           microkernels and cache-sized rounds. This is a more general version
           of multi-output fusion than a fixed GEN1..GEN6 family.

Jerasure/GF: Matrix construction and region arithmetic are separated. This is
             flexible and makes each coefficient operation simple, but does not
             naturally provide the same fixed small-parity fusion as SnapRAID.

CM256/gf256: Each recovery block is a Cauchy matrix row evaluated by
             whole-region GF operations. The first all-one row can reduce to
             XOR; remaining rows use generic coefficient multiply-add.


Recovery strategy
-----------------

Recovery comparisons must separate the common single-failure case from rare
multi-failure cases. A sophisticated general decoder can be useful without
improving the normal SnapRAID recovery path.

SnapRAID: With one missing data disk and P available, rec1of1 remaps pointers
          and uses the XOR generator. No temporary syndrome buffer, GF multiply
          or matrix solve is required. Generic REC1/REC2/RECX handle less
          common parity selections and multiple failures.

* Generic recovery first obtains the needed delta/syndrome information, builds
  and inverts only the small N by N matrix for failed data, then runs
  specialized SIMD recovery kernels.

* N is at most six, so scalar matrix inversion setup is negligible compared
  with processing 256 KiB blocks.

Linux RAID6: Uses formulas specific to the RAID6 failure cases. Two-data
             recovery temporarily substitutes zero pages, generates delta P/Q
             using the normal syndrome engine, then solves the two equations
             with precomputed multipliers. Data+P recovery has its own formula.

* This is an algorithmic specialization made possible by exactly two parity
  equations.

* The recovery API limit of PAGE_SIZE makes additional passes much less costly
  than they would be over a full SnapRAID block.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Dispatches directly among rec_p, rec_q, rec_r, rec_pq, rec_pr,
               rec_qr and rec_pqr according to missing data count and available
               parity. Recovery coefficients are calculated before the SIMD
               loops.

* The implementation computes syndromes from available columns and then applies
  explicit one-, two- or three-failure equations.

* Big/short RAIDZ column geometry can split the reconstruction into ranges so
  conditional logic is kept out of the SIMD inner loops.

ISA-L: Provides the GF matrix inversion and fast dot-product primitives needed
       for decode, but the core fast path is intentionally generic: construct
       decode coefficients and run the same multi-output coding engine on
       surviving sources.

klauspost: Selects surviving shards, constructs and inverts the decoding
           matrix, then invokes the same multi-output coding machinery for the
           requested missing shards. An inversion-tree cache can reuse previous
           erasure patterns.

Jerasure/GF: Selects surviving rows, constructs a decoding matrix and inverts
             it for generic matrix decode. Bitmatrix modes can instead build
             XOR schedules, including smart schedules intended to reduce XOR
             operations.

CM256/gf256: Uses the algebraic structure of its Cauchy matrix and a direct
             Schur-type LDU solve. This avoids treating the decode matrix as a
             completely unstructured large matrix, but the triangular solve
             performs region multiply-add operations over the recovery blocks.

The CM256 direct Cauchy solver is mathematically interesting if SnapRAID ever
grows far beyond six parity levels. For the current N <= 6 domain, reducing
matrix-setup complexity is not valuable enough to compensate for an unfavorable
large-block traversal.


SIMD and register strategy
--------------------------

SnapRAID: Uses parity-count-specific register allocation. Current recovery
          keeps delta vectors in registers, reuses pre-split nibbles, and
          precomputes coefficient table pointers or GFNI affine representations
          before the hot loop. Current GEN2/GEN3 can keep multiplication tables
          resident in vector registers where space permits.

* REC1 has 64-byte spatial unrolling on selected AVX2/SSSE3/NEON paths.

* NEON REC2 uses two 16-byte blocks per iteration.

* AVX512 width/unroll is benchmark-driven; wider 128-byte processing is not
  assumed to be faster.

Linux RAID6: Uses fixed P/Q register state and multiple spatial-unroll
             variants. AVX2 and AVX512 implementations explicitly prefetch
             source data and use simple fixed MUL2 arithmetic rather than
             arbitrary coefficient tables during generation.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Backend macro layers operate on multiple vector registers per
               primitive and provide fixed MUL2/MUL4 plus general coefficient
               multiplication. The surrounding algorithm is designed around 64
               KiB cache chunks and ABD iteration rather than keeping parity
               live across every data column.

ISA-L: The 1..6 output kernels are a strong reference for generic multi-output
       fusion. AVX2 can load one source, form low/high nibble indices once, and
       apply several coefficient tables to several destination accumulators.

* GFNI removes the nibble-shuffle multiply itself, but output-count and
  register-pressure tradeoffs remain. AVX2 GFNI and AVX512 GFNI therefore do
  not necessarily use identical maximum output group sizes.

* Current SVE/SVE2 code can pre-detect an all-one coefficient output and use
  direct XOR instead of table multiplication.

klauspost: Generated NxM kernels use the register file for a broader generic
           matrix tile. The trade is much more generated code in exchange for
           specialized input/output combinations and fewer generic loops in the
           hot path.

Jerasure/GF: GF-Complete region kernels are optimized around one coefficient at
             a time. The coefficient tables can remain resident for the entire
             region, which minimizes setup but provides less simultaneous
             output fusion.

CM256/gf256: gf256 similarly optimizes region operations around one
             coefficient. Its AVX2 representation can store already duplicated
             vector-width low/high tables so the hot loop uses normal 256-bit
             loads instead of broadcasts.


Coefficient and table handling
------------------------------

Coefficient setup is one of the areas where several projects converge on the
same principle: move address calculation, table expansion and matrix work out
of the byte-processing loop whenever possible.

SnapRAID non-GFNI RECX: Precomputes direct pointers to compact 2x16-byte
                        shuffle tables for every inverse-matrix coefficient.
                        The asm therefore avoids coefficient-byte index
                        arithmetic in the hot loop.

SnapRAID RAID GFNI RECX: Precomputes the complete 8-byte affine matrix
                         representation of every inverse coefficient, removing
                         the coefficient-to-global-table dependency from the
                         hot loop.

ISA-L: ec_init_tables expands every coefficient to a 32-byte representation
       before encode/decode. The same high-level idea is used for both classic
       shuffle kernels and GFNI-ready constants.

GF-Complete: A region operation selects the coefficient tables once and reuses
             them across the whole region.

gf256: The standalone AVX2 path keeps 256-bit pre-expanded low/high tables.
       Compared with a compact 2x16-byte representation, this removes
       broadcasts but doubles bytes fetched per coefficient and increases table
       footprint.

For SnapRAID RECX, pre-expanding every AVX2 coefficient table to 2x32 bytes
remains only a microbenchmark idea, not an assumed improvement. The larger
representation doubles coefficient-table traffic. For AVX512 the footprint
penalty becomes even less attractive.


Memory, cache and store behavior
--------------------------------

SnapRAID: Large source blocks and queued parity favor streaming behavior.
          Generation normally writes parity once with non-temporal stores.
          Keeping many generated parity blocks resident in cache is not the
          objective.

Linux RAID6: Optimized generation is very similar: source prefetch and
             non-temporal final P/Q stores are used in x86 paths. The separate
             xor_syndrome update operation reads and modifies existing parity
             and therefore uses normal cached stores.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Cached stores are integral to the traversal. P/Q/R are
               intentionally loaded and updated again as later data columns are
               processed inside the 64 KiB cache block. Non-temporal stores
               would fight that reuse pattern.

ISA-L: The public erasure-code API is a memory-buffer library and is not tied
       to a disk-writer queue. x86 assembly includes different
       aligned/unaligned load/store configurations, but its store policy should
       not be copied into SnapRAID independently of the surrounding traversal
       and consumer.

klauspost: Cache-aware rounds are a first-class part of the generic
           implementation. The library attempts to size active input/output
           work to cache rather than assuming storage-style streaming parity
           output.

Jerasure/GF: Whole-region operations naturally use cached source/destination
             buffers and may revisit them for other matrix rows.

CM256/gf256: Whole-region multiply and multiply-add operations also assume
             normal memory reuse. This is appropriate for a generic codec but
             does not match SnapRAID final parity lifetime as closely as Linux
             RAID6 does.

OpenZFS is the clearest demonstration that store policy cannot be judged in
isolation: cached stores are correct for an algorithm that immediately reloads
parity, while SnapRAID MOVNT is appropriate when final parity is not consumed
until later in a queue.


Temporary memory and working state
----------------------------------

SnapRAID: Common rec1of1 uses no syndrome temporary. Generic recovery has small
          N-by-N setup state and delta/syndrome buffers, with N <= 6. Current
          RECX tries to keep vector deltas and their nibble splits in
          registers.

Linux RAID6: Two-disk recovery reuses the failed data pages themselves as
             temporary delta P and delta Q storage, avoiding a separate large
             allocation.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Works through ABDs and target/syndrome buffers whose geometry
               may differ between big and short columns. Its working unit is
               intentionally split to keep the SIMD loops regular.

ISA-L: Requires caller-provided expanded coefficient tables of 32*k*rows bytes
       for fast encode/decode, in addition to the source and destination
       buffers.

klauspost: Maintains matrix/decode setup state and cache-oriented work
           partitioning. Recovery can also retain inversion information for
           repeated erasure patterns.

Jerasure/GF: Bitmatrix and schedule modes can introduce schedule data and
             packetized intermediate work. Direct matrix/GF paths have
             coefficient and matrix setup state plus the data regions.

CM256/gf256: The direct Cauchy solve primarily transforms recovery blocks with
             region operations and coefficient setup rather than allocating a
             large generic decoding matrix for each data byte.


Special cases and fast paths
----------------------------

SnapRAID: P is a dedicated XOR path. rec1of1 with P available reduces to GEN1.
          Coefficient 0/1 simplifications are useful when they can be
          established without adding harmful hot-loop branches.

Linux RAID6: The entire implementation is a fixed special case: P+Q only.
             Single failure can use RAID5-style reconstruction, while two-data
             and data+P failure have explicit formulas.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Has explicit recovery functions for every P/Q/R combination
               through three missing data columns. The dispatcher selects the
               cheapest usable parity set.

ISA-L: Current SVE/SVE2 recognizes coefficient-one destinations and replaces GF
       lookup multiplication with XOR. Other paths benefit from fixed
       1..6-output kernel families rather than RAID-specific formulas.

klauspost: Can select generated special kernels based on matrix/output shape
           and contains matrix-property-aware paths such as all-one first
           parity rows on some generated backends.

Jerasure/GF: GF-Complete handles coefficient 0 and 1 specially once per region.
             Jerasure can exploit all-one rows and bitmatrix schedules at a
             higher coding level.

CM256/gf256: The normalized first recovery row is XOR. gf256 region
             multiply-add also special-cases trivial coefficients outside the
             inner byte loop.


Incremental parity and update APIs
----------------------------------

SnapRAID: The RAID library hot generation path computes complete parity blocks
          for the selected stripe/block. A generic single-source MAD update API
          is not the main optimization target.

Linux RAID6: raid6_xor_syndrome explicitly supports in-place P/Q parity
             updates. The old contribution can be removed, data changed, and
             the new contribution added using the same syndrome structure.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: The RAIDZ math engine is integrated with copy-on-write
               filesystem row generation/reconstruction rather than exposed as
               a generic single-source erasure-code update primitive.

ISA-L: ec_encode_data_update and the gf_*vect_mad family are explicit
       single-source update operations that can update several destinations.

klauspost: The library supports broader shard update/reconstruction workflows,
           but its main optimized coding engine remains generic matrix coding
           rather than a storage-specific parity-delta API.

Jerasure/GF: GF region multiply with add semantics can be used to accumulate a
             single source contribution, although the higher-level matrix API
             is not built around SnapRAID-style block synchronization.

CM256/gf256: gf256 multiply-add is naturally usable as an update primitive,
             while the CM256 encoding API normally constructs complete recovery
             blocks.


Runtime dispatch and calibration
--------------------------------

SnapRAID: Selects implementations from CPU capabilities and the configured
          backend. Broad startup microbenchmark calibration is intentionally
          not part of the normal design.

Linux RAID6: Can benchmark registered generation algorithms at boot using
             PAGE_SIZE buffers and select the fastest result. Recovery
             selection is more directly tied to architecture capability
             priority.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: Benchmarks generation and recovery operations independently and
               builds a fastest implementation whose gen_p, gen_pq, gen_pqr and
               recovery functions may come from different SIMD backends.

ISA-L: Uses multibinary runtime dispatch to choose the best implementation for
       the detected instruction-set capabilities.

klauspost: Detects CPU capabilities and chooses generated SIMD functions, cache
           strategy and parallel work according to the active platform/options.

Jerasure/GF: GF-Complete initializes a field implementation appropriate for the
             selected technique and available SIMD capabilities rather than
             microbenchmarking all alternatives for each operation.

CM256/gf256: gf256 selects available SIMD support during initialization. The
             codec does not use a Linux/OpenZFS-style per-operation startup
             benchmark.

Linux and OpenZFS are useful references for runtime benchmarking, but their
existence does not by itself justify adding calibration to SnapRAID. SnapRAID
has fewer known kernels and a stable large-block workload; calibration adds
startup work, complexity and another source of platform-dependent behavior.


Scalability and implementation constraints
------------------------------------------

SnapRAID: One to six parity levels. This small fixed N is a central
          optimization assumption: it bounds register pressure, recovery matrix
          size and the number of specialized kernels.

Linux RAID6: Exactly two parity levels. It is the most specialized
             implementation in the comparison and therefore the least suitable
             model for parity counts above Q.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: One to three parity levels. The implementation is specialized
               around P/Q/R rather than arbitrary Reed-Solomon parity counts.

ISA-L: Generic matrix coding. Fast functions fuse a bounded number of outputs
       at a time, and larger parity counts are decomposed into several kernel
       groups.

klauspost: Normal GF(2^8) Reed-Solomon supports the usual byte-field shard
           regime; Leopard provides a different path for much larger shard
           counts, up to tens of thousands.

Jerasure/GF: General matrix/bitmatrix framework with field-width choices beyond
             the fixed small-parity RAID engines. Its algorithms prioritize
             generality over a six-parity register budget.

CM256/gf256: Designed for a total original+recovery block count within the
             GF(2^8) index space and is comfortable with substantially more
             recovery blocks than SnapRAID.

This explains why structured solvers, schedule caches or O(n log n) coding can
be important elsewhere while being unnecessary for current SnapRAID. With N <=
6, arithmetic setup complexity is tiny relative to moving the data.


What each implementation teaches SnapRAID
-----------------------------------------

Linux RAID6: The strongest reference for P+Q streaming generation. It confirms
             single-pass source traversal, live parity accumulators,
             fixed-generator Horner arithmetic and non-temporal final stores as
             sensible storage techniques. Its x1/x2/x4 unrolling and prefetch
             are ideas to benchmark, not assumptions to copy.

OpenZFS RAIDZ: The strongest counterexample for memory policy. Its 64 KiB L2
               blocking and cached parity stores are coherent because parity is
               deliberately reused after every data column. The same technique
               would increase SnapRAID parity traffic and conflicts with the
               queued final-output lifetime.

ISA-L: The strongest generic reference for multi-output SIMD coding. It
       demonstrates that source reuse, nibble reuse and several live output
       accumulators are useful even without RAID-specific matrices. GFNI Horner
       and coefficient precomputation ideas have already influenced SnapRAID.

klauspost: Shows how far a generic Reed-Solomon engine can push specialization
           through generated NxM kernels, cache-aware rounds and parallelism.
           It is a useful contrast to SnapRAID hand-specializing GEN1..GEN6
           around known RAID semantics.

Jerasure/GF: Provides historical matrix, Cauchy and GF implementation
             references. Its coefficient residency is useful to understand, but
             packetized smart schedules, good-Cauchy bitmatrix one-count
             minimization and repeated whole-region passes do not map directly
             to current SnapRAID SIMD coding.

CM256/gf256: Provides a compact Cauchy codec reference. The direct Cauchy LDU
             solver is interesting for a hypothetical much larger parity count.
             Its pre-expanded AVX2 shuffle tables are a clean microbenchmark
             idea, but their larger footprint is not automatically favorable
             for RECX.


Ideas already represented in current SnapRAID
---------------------------------------------

* Fuse several parity outputs so a source chunk can update all of them before
  it is discarded.

* Use fixed-generator Horner arithmetic where the matrix structure and backend
  make it advantageous.

* Use low/high nibble shuffle multiplication for general GF(2^8) coefficients
  on appropriate SIMD backends.

* Use GFNI with precomputed coefficient representations when hardware support
  makes it profitable.

* Move coefficient address/matrix setup out of the hot data loop.

* Keep recovery deltas and reused nibble splits in registers where register
  pressure permits it.

* Spatially unroll simple kernels where independent chains improve throughput
  without creating excessive register pressure.

* Use a direct XOR fast path for P and for the common one-data recovery case
  when P is available.

* Use non-temporal final parity stores for the normal large-block generation
  pipeline.


Ideas worth keeping as references
---------------------------------

* Linux-style alternative spatial unroll factors are useful benchmark
  references for specific kernels, but wider is not assumed faster.

* Linux/OpenZFS runtime benchmarking is a reference if future CPU diversity
  makes static dispatch insufficient.

* ISA-L and klauspost multi-output kernels remain good references for register
  allocation and output grouping, especially as new GFNI/SVE backends evolve.

* ISA-L coefficient-one detection is worth considering only where the
  coefficient can be classified outside the hot loop and the resulting
  branching/register cost is favorable.

* gf256 pre-expanded AVX2 shuffle tables are a small, isolated RECX
  microbenchmark candidate. They should be tested against the extra L1 table
  traffic, not accepted from instruction count alone.

* CM256 structured Cauchy LDU is a useful future reference if SnapRAID ever
  supports parity counts large enough for decoding-matrix setup to matter
  materially.

* klauspost cache tiling and generated NxM specialization are useful
  architectural references if SnapRAID ever moves toward much larger parity
  counts or more generic coding matrices.


Ideas that do not transfer directly
-----------------------------------

* Do not adopt a source/output loop orientation that requires another full pass
  over each 256 KiB source merely to keep one coefficient table resident
  longer.

* Do not adopt OpenZFS cached parity update loops without also adopting the
  cache-blocked repeated-parity-reuse workload that makes them sensible.

* Do not replace direct byte-wise GF SIMD with Jerasure smart bitmatrix
  schedules merely because those schedules reduce XOR count. Their cost model
  includes packetization and memory traffic that SnapRAID does not currently
  need.

* Do not change the SnapRAID extended Cauchy matrix to a Jerasure good-Cauchy
  matrix just to reduce bitmatrix one-count. That metric does not reduce
  PSHUFB/GFNI instruction count for a general coefficient.

* Do not introduce a large generic field object or generic matrix framework
  when fixed <=6 parity specialization provides simpler hot paths.

* Do not trade register pressure for a second full source pass unless a real
  large-block benchmark proves that the saved arithmetic is larger than the
  added memory traffic.

* Do not assume pre-expanded vector-width coefficient tables are faster. They
  replace broadcasts with extra table bytes and can consume a large fraction of
  L1, especially at AVX512 width.

* Do not optimize recovery-matrix setup at the expense of extra 256 KiB data
  passes while the matrix dimension remains at most six.


Overall conclusions
-------------------

No external implementation is a universally better model for SnapRAID. The
useful comparison is to identify which project is closest for a particular
design question.

    P+Q streaming and final stores:
        Linux RAID6

    Filesystem/cache-blocked RAID with repeated parity reuse:
        OpenZFS RAIDZ

    Modern generic multi-output SIMD erasure coding:
        Intel ISA-L

    Generated generic NxM kernels and cache-aware Reed-Solomon:
        klauspost/reedsolomon

    General coding matrices, bitmatrices and historical GF techniques:
        Jerasure/GF-Complete

    Compact Cauchy codec and structured Cauchy decoding:
        CM256/gf256

The current SnapRAID engine occupies a distinct point in this design space: it
is more general than Linux RAID6 and OpenZFS RAIDZ in parity count, but much
more specialized than ISA-L, klauspost, Jerasure or CM256 in coding matrix and
workload.

That specialization is valuable because SnapRAID normally processes large 256
KiB regions. The dominant design objective is therefore not merely to minimize
GF instructions. It is to combine low arithmetic cost with low memory traffic:
read large sources as few times as possible, advance all required parity
together, keep reusable state in registers, and write final parity once.

When evaluating a new external optimization, first ask whether it preserves
those properties. If it does not, the burden of proof is a full SnapRAID
large-block benchmark, not a smaller microkernel instruction count.
